# Mainroom

https://mainroom.sh
https://github.com/cs50victor/mainroom

Mainroom lets friends and research groups pool subscription inference capacity
and share costs, even when members contribute or consume different amounts.
Each person keeps one endpoint, such as https://victor.mainroom.sh/v1/responses,
backed by their own accounts and nodes shared with them. Providers control access
and usage limits; the group agrees how to split subscription costs.

The CLI handles sign-in and uploads selected Codex authentication files. The
backend combines those accounts and incoming peer grants in each node's
tokenproxy configuration. Mutual sharing uses a separate grant in each direction;
friends use their own Mainroom keys without exchanging subscription credentials.

## Architecture

- Cloudflare Worker: src/worker.ts handles public control-plane routes,
  authentication, sharing, machine management, and request forwarding.
- Cloudflare Durable Objects: store user machine records and sharing grants.
  UserMachineContainer manages Fly Machines despite its historical name.
- Cloudflare Container: Dockerfile runs the Bun/Hono API in src/index.ts.
  The Worker forwards fallback requests, including /health, to this service.
- Fly.io Machines: run tokenproxy v0.1.16, one machine per user, in the
  mainroom-user-machines app. Dockerfile.user-machine packages the binary.
  Machines use port 8787, default to one shared CPU and 256 MB RAM, and are
  configured to suspend when idle and start on incoming traffic.
- Cloudflare R2/S3: stores uploaded authentication JSON files.
- Clerk: provides user authentication and CLI OAuth sign-in.

User inference requests follow this path:

<username>.mainroom.sh -> Cloudflare Worker -> user's Fly Machine -> upstream

The Worker selects the user's machine with Fly's fly-force-instance-id header.
On every boot, the machine uses its existing client credential to fetch fresh
configuration from /v0/tokenproxy/config/me into a private local file, then
starts tokenproxy with that file. The endpoint derives the user from the
credential. Existing machines receive the new startup command in place.
See src/fly-machines.ts and src/worker.ts for lifecycle and routing.

## Connecting and recovering Codex accounts

mainroom auth signup
mainroom codex sync
mainroom codex status
mainroom codex reauth --account <filename.json>
mainroom codex disable <filename.json>

Mainroom sign-in identifies your Mainroom account. Codex sign-in authorizes the
upstream account used by your remote tokenproxy. Renewing a Mainroom login does
not renew a rejected Codex credential.

codex status checks each stored account even when tokenproxy cannot start.
It reports ready, reauth_required, disabled, invalid, or unavailable. Only an
upstream 401 is classified as requiring reauth; network errors, rate limits,
and other upstream failures remain unavailable. Status does not run inference.

codex reauth uses the official Codex CLI, which must be installed locally.
It opens a fresh sign-in session in a private temporary directory, checks the
selected account identity, and uploads the refreshed credential to the same
remote filename. Your usual local Codex login is preserved. Use --device-auth
for device-code sign-in. With multiple stored accounts, choose one interactively
or pass --account. A disabled account is enabled again after validation.

One invalid enabled account can prevent tokenproxy from starting. codex disable
keeps that account's credential but excludes it from future configurations.
It stops the current machine, including any suspended snapshot, so a previous
configuration cannot keep using the disabled account. This interrupts active
requests. Use --yes to skip the confirmation in scripts.

After sync, reauth, or disable, Mainroom checks every enabled account, reloads
the remote configuration, and verifies a completed inference response using a
Codex-supported model. If another account needs attention, it names the account
and provides recovery commands. If no accounts remain enabled, it leaves the
machine stopped and reports that an account must be connected. A saved credential
or an HTTP 200 alone is not reported as successful inference.

Users do not need the tokenproxy CLI locally. The remote machine runs it.

## Development and deployment

The homepage serves an agent setup prompt directly; /docs remains the interactive
API reference. Public guides live at /guides, and /SKILL.md contains the setup
instructions. Each guide supports a .md URL and Accept: text/markdown.
/llms.txt, /llms-full.txt, /sitemap.xml, /robots.txt, and /.well-known/api-catalog
provide discovery and exports. These public routes run in both the Worker and Bun
server; Worker requests do not need to wake the container to read them.

Page components live in src/site as server-rendered Hono TSX. Tailwind CSS and
its typography plugin provide the styling; src/site/tailwind.css contains only
Tailwind directives and theme tokens. Compiled CSS in src/generated is ignored
by Git and rebuilt for local startup, Docker, CI, and Wrangler deployment.

Edit the Markdown sources in src/content to update both HTML guides and exports.
The generated OpenAPI covers container API routes; src/content/api.md documents
the additional Worker routes. Keep guide examples aligned with the CLI and route
implementations. Private API paths and user subdomains are excluded from crawling;
authentication, not robots.txt, protects private content.

Use Bun; CI pins Bun 1.3.14. Copy .env.example to .env and configure the
services needed for local work. Keep credentials out of version control.

bun install --frozen-lockfile
bun run dev
bun run build:css
bun run typecheck
bun run lint
bun test

bun run dev watches both Tailwind sources and the Bun API. It does not start
the complete Worker/Fly deployment.
Production Fly settings and required Worker secrets are in wrangler.jsonc;
.env.example does not list every production requirement.

Pushes to main run CI. Successful CI on main triggers the Deploy workflow,
which ensures proxied DNS records, deploys the Worker and Cloudflare Container,
and runs smoke checks. The Fly tokenproxy image is configured separately in
wrangler.jsonc and built from Dockerfile.user-machine.

## Outage investigation: September 6, 2026, approximately 05:15 UTC

These findings are a dated snapshot, not a live service status.

- Cloudflare's API reported that the mainroom Worker did not exist and that
  mainroom.sh had no Worker routes. The dashboard also lacked the Worker.
- The root and wildcard DNS records still pointed to proxied AAAA 100::.
  This is the placeholder created by .github/scripts/ensure-cloudflare-dns.sh;
  it depends on the Worker intercepting requests. Without the Worker and its
  routes, requests fall through to that placeholder and return HTTP 522.
- Both Google and Cloudflare DNS resolvers agreed. Direct probes of both
  advertised Cloudflare IPv4 addresses returned 522 for the root and the
  Worker-only /v0/auth/cli/config endpoint. HTTPS and HTTP-to-HTTPS redirection
  worked; /cdn-cgi/trace returned 200. Root, health, ping, and user inference
  probes failed, while clerk.mainroom.sh returned 200.
- A direct Fly probe woke the existing user machine. Its logs showed that
  tokenproxy failed to fetch its configuration from mainroom.sh with HTTP 522,
  exited with code 1, and left the machine stopped.
- The saved Fly startup configuration URL expired June 23, 2026 at 13:32:10
  UTC. The code issues these URLs for ten minutes and rejects expired URLs
  with 401. Existing-machine startup does not replace the saved startup URL,
  so restoring the Worker alone does not resolve that restart problem.
- The last successful deployment was June 23, 2026, for commit 8b26ff7:
  https://github.com/cs50victor/mainroom/actions/runs/28029613753
- Wrangler and cloudflared were installed. Wrangler's saved OAuth login had
  expired, but the project Cloudflare API token and Fly CLI authentication
  worked. The Cloudflare token lacked audit-log access, so the time, actor,
  and reason behind the Worker's disappearance remain unknown.

Recovery began September 6, 2026. Restore the Worker, routes, secrets, and required
bindings; check retained Durable Object state before reconnecting existing
Fly machines. Address the expired startup configuration URL, then verify
Worker-only routes, /health, machine startup, and authenticated inference.
Audit-log read access is needed to investigate the Worker's disappearance.
